.TH UBERTOOTH\-UTIL 1 "October 2026" "Project Ubertooth" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
.PP
.BR ubertooth-util (1) 
\- general purpose Ubertooth utility
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
ubertooth\-util [\-v] [\-V] [\-H] [\-\-host\-version] [\-I] [\-d[0\-1]] [\-l[0\-1]] [\-S] [\-r] [\-U<index|serial|all>] [\-a[0\-7]] [\-c[2400\-2483]] [\-C[0\-78]] [\-q[1\-225]] [\-t] [\-z] [\-e] [\-m] [\-n] [\-f] [\-i] [\-b] [\-p] [\-s] [\-N] [\-x] [\-h]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
.BR ubertooth-util (1) 
is a catch\-all tool for doing generally useful things
with, to, and about Ubertooth. To get firmware revision and compile
information, run:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ubertooth\-util \-vV
.fi
.RE
.PP
To fully reset Ubertooth, run:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ubertooth\-util \-r
.fi
.RE
.PP
Other options are available for modifying LED states, setting channel,
and performing miscellaneous radio\-related actions. Refer to the
[OPTIONS][] section for full details.
.PP
The utility also includes a simple range test tool for use with two
Uberteeth. Refer to [RANGE TEST][] for full details.
.PP
Operations that put the device into a continuous mode \- \fB\fC\-I\fR, \fB\fC\-q\fR,
\fB\fC\-x\fR, \fB\fC\-t\fR, \fB\fC\-n\fR, and \fB\fC\-e\fR \- first stop any operation
already running on that device, so each starts from a known idle state. This
resets only the operating mode; the configured channel and power level are
preserved.
.SH OPTIONS
.PP
Common options:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-v\fR :
get firmware revision number; exits non\-zero if the read fails
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-V\fR :
get compile info; exits non\-zero if the read fails
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-H\fR, \fB\fC\-\-host\-version\fR :
get host tools (libubertooth) version; works without a device
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-I\fR :
identify ubertooth device by flashing the USR, RX, and TX LEDs (runs until Ctrl-C)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-d[0\-1]\fR :
get/set all LEDs
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-l[0\-1]\fR :
get/set USR LED
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-S\fR :
stop current operation
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-r\fR :
full reset
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-U<index|serial>\fR :
select which Ubertooth to use, either by USB enumeration index or by its serial number string; see SELECTING A DEVICE
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-U all\fR :
report the read\-only queries \fB\fC\-b\fR, \fB\fC\-v\fR, \fB\fC\-V\fR, \fB\fC\-p\fR and \fB\fC\-s\fR for every attached Ubertooth; refused with any other option; see SELECTING A DEVICE
.RE
.PP
Radio options:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-a[0\-7]\fR :
get/set power amplifier level
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-c[2400\-2483]\fR :
get/set channel in MHz
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-C[0\-78]\fR :
get/set channel
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-q[1\-225 (RSSI threshold)]\fR :
start LED spectrum analyzer
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-t\fR :
intitiate continuous transmit test
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-z\fR :
set squelch level
.RE
.PP
Range test:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-e\fR :
start repeater mode
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-m\fR :
display range test result
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-n\fR :
initiate range test
.RE
.PP
Miscellaneous:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-f\fR :
activate flash programming (DFU) mode
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-i\fR :
activate In\-System Programming (ISP) mode
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-b\fR :
get hardware board id number
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-p\fR :
get microcontroller Part ID (32\-bit, hexadecimal); exits non\-zero if the
device reports a nonzero status for the read
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-s\fR :
get microcontroller serial number
.RE
.SH SELECTING A DEVICE
.PP
With a single Ubertooth attached, the tools find it automatically and \fB\fC\-U\fR
is unnecessary.
.PP
With multiple Ubertooths attached, \fB\fC\-U\fR selects one either by its
position in the USB device list or by its serial number. \fB\fC\-U<n>\fR
selects by index (\fB\fC\-U0\fR is the first match, and so on); this index
reflects USB enumeration order (bus/port and plug order) \- it is not derived
from the serial number and is not guaranteed stable across reboots or
re\-plugging. \fB\fC\-U<serial>\fR selects the device whose serial number
matches the given string, which is stable across reboots and re\-plugging and
is the recommended way to pin a specific board.
.PP
The argument may be attached or separated \- \fB\fC\-U0\fR and \fB\fC\-U 0\fR are
equivalent, as are \fB\fC\-U<serial>\fR and \fB\fC\-U <serial>\fR. An all\-digits
argument is treated as an index, anything else as a serial. There is no fixed
upper limit on attached devices (older docs showed \fB\fC\-U<0\-7>\fR, a
convention only); with many devices prefer serial selection.
.PP
With several Ubertooths attached and no \fB\fC\-U\fR, the read\-only identity
queries \fB\fC\-s\fR (serial), \fB\fC\-v\fR (firmware version), \fB\fC\-V\fR (compile
info), \fB\fC\-p\fR (part ID) and \fB\fC\-b\fR (board ID) report every device, in
enumeration order, instead of refusing to pick one. A single query prints one
\fB\fC<index>: <value>\fR line per device:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ubertooth\-util \-s
0: 1cc001038088cb956c43ad61c52000f5
1: 05f00014e380df95f4436562c32000f5
.fi
.RE
.PP
Several queries together (for example \fB\fC\-vVspb\fR) print each report line
prefixed with the device index. A device that cannot be opened is shown as
\fB\fC<index>: <unavailable>\fR, a failed read as \fB\fC<index>: <query read
failed>\fR, and a query that reports an error as that error (for example
\fB\fC<error: unknown board ID 7>\fR). The other devices are still reported, and
the command exits non\-zero if any device failed. A failed \fB\fC\-v\fR or
\fB\fC\-V\fR read is shown as \fB\fC<index>: <firmware read failed>\fR or
\fB\fC<index>: <compile info read failed>\fR; a firmware version whose API
version cannot be read is shown without it and also makes the command fail.
Listing does not stop a mode a device is running. Any other option in the
same command still needs \fB\fC\-U\fR\&.
.PP
\fB\fC\-U all\fR asks for the same listing explicitly. It works with any number
of attached devices, including one (\fB\fC0: <value>\fR), and fails with
\fB\fCerror: no Ubertooth devices found\fR when none is attached:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ubertooth\-util \-U all \-s
0: 1cc001038088cb956c43ad61c52000f5
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fB\fC\-U all\fR only applies to the read\-only queries \fB\fC\-b\fR, \fB\fC\-v\fR,
\fB\fC\-V\fR, \fB\fC\-p\fR and \fB\fC\-s\fR\&. Combined with any other option (a
setter, a mode such as \fB\fC\-x\fR or \fB\fC\-q\fR, \fB\fC\-r\fR, \fB\fC\-S\fR,
\fB\fC\-N\fR, or another read such as \fB\fC\-c\fR), or given with no query at
all, it is refused before any device is opened:
.PP
.RS
.nf
error: \-U all only supports the read\-only queries \-b, \-v, \-V, \-p and \-s; select one device with \-U<index|serial> for other options
.fi
.RE
.PP
Only the exact word \fB\fCall\fR has this meaning. The other tools do not
support \fB\fC\-U all\fR; they treat it as a serial number and report that no
device matches.
.PP
To tell which index or serial is which physical device: run
\fB\fCubertooth\-util \-s\fR, or any other tool with no \fB\fC\-U\fR (with more than
one present it lists each index with its serial number), or flash the LEDs on a given device and watch which board blinks:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ubertooth\-util \-U0 \-I
.fi
.RE
.PP
For a stable identity across reboots, select a device by its serial number
(\fB\fC\-U<serial> \-I\fR); unlike the index, the serial does not change.
.SH RANGE TEST
.PP
Using two Uberteeth it is possible to perform a range test. One
Ubertooth acts as a repeater by running:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ubertooth\-util \-e
.fi
.RE
.PP
A second Ubertooth (the sender) initiates the range test by running:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ubertooth\-util \-n
.fi
.RE
.PP
During the range test the sender Ubertooth will transmit data packets at
increasing power level, listening for repeats from the repeater
Ubertooth. The receiving Ubertooth will validate that the received data
was not corrupted in flight.
.PP
The range test results can be displayed on the sender Ubertooth's system
by running:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ubertooth\-util \-m
.fi
.RE
.PP
Note that the range test transmits the Cortex M3 microcontroller's
serial number. If you do not wish to broadcast that information
wirelessly, do not use the range test functionality.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.PP
\fB\fCubertooth\-util\fR exits with 0 when every requested operation succeeds, including
queries that report a nonzero value such as \fB\fC\-b\fR (board ID), \fB\fC\-c\fR (channel), or
\fB\fC\-a\fR (power amplifier level). It exits with a nonzero status if any requested
operation fails, even when a later operation in the same command succeeds.
